From: ai-hakzarov <226896199+ai-hakzarov@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 21:19:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] 9761: Decompiler: the default lane size guess reads the lanes
 its storage already uses

A vector value that is only loaded and stored whole gives the lane split
no lane size of its own, so the default guess divides it by the pointer
size. When the value is copied into a stack location that the function
accesses in smaller lanes, that guess is wrong and shapes the location's
symbols into pointer-size halves, so every real lane then prints as a
piece of them.

The guess now reads the storage first. Before the stack has been through
heritage the location is still reached through the stack pointer plus a
constant, so the STOREs of the value and the LOAD defining it name the
location, and the other LOADs and STOREs through the stack pointer into it
are its lanes (RuleLoadVarnode::checkSpacebase is made public for this).
After heritage the value sits in an address-tied Varnode, reached directly
or through a COPY or a call's INDIRECT, and the smaller Varnodes inside its
range are the lanes. The largest allowed lane size that tiles every access
is tried before the default. A lane size equal to the whole register is
skipped: one lane is no division.

The slotlanes datatest builds such a stack vector lane by lane, loads it
whole into a register between calls, rewrites three lanes and overwrites
it with one 16-byte copy whose address a call takes; it reads as six
floats, with no pointer-size halves.
